Q: Dynamically create R6 methods with a for loop A: <p>This is a FAQ. Look at this:</p>
<pre><code>object &lt;- SimpleClass$new()

environment(object$a)
#&lt;environment: 0x0000021511c64060&gt;
environment(object$b)
#&lt;environment: 0x0000021511c64060&gt;

environment(object$a)$i
#[1] 10
environment(object$a)$letter
#[1] &quot;j&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Your <a href="https://cran.r-project.org/doc/manuals/r-patched/R-lang.html#Function-objects" rel="nofollow noreferrer">closures</a> all share the same environment. When they are called, <code>letter</code> has the value from the last iteration of the loop.</p>
<p>Your last approach ensures that each closure has its own environment with the right value of <code>letter</code>:</p>
<pre><code>environment(object$a)
#&lt;environment: 0x000002150bcbeeb0&gt;
environment(object$b)
#&lt;environment: 0x000002150bcb68f8&gt;
environment(object$a)$i
#NULL
environment(object$a)$letter
#[1] &quot;a&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>You can avoid the scoping for <code>letter</code> by computing on the language:</p>
<pre><code>SimpleClass &lt;- R6::R6Class(
  &quot;SimpleClass&quot;,
  public = list(
    initialize = function() {
      for (i in 1:10) {
        letter &lt;- letters[i]
        f &lt;- function(x) paste(x, &quot;-&quot;, letter)
        body(f) &lt;-  substitute(paste(x, &quot;-&quot;, letter))
        self[[letter]] &lt;- f
      }
    }
  ),
  lock_objects = FALSE
)
object &lt;- SimpleClass$new()

object$a
#function (x) 
#paste(x, &quot;-&quot;, &quot;a&quot;)
#&lt;environment: 0x0000020990e68f80&gt;

object$a(&quot;f&quot;)
#[1] &quot;f - a&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: Correct syntax for function template with an even number of `int` arguments? A: <p>Following @Jarod42's answer, it is possible to avoid one level of <code>{}</code> at call site, i.e. one could have:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int main ()
{
    // Expecting 0.0234
    printf (&quot;x = %f\n&quot;, xfun (0.1, {2,2}, {3,3}, {4,4}));

    // Expecting 0.56
    printf (&quot;x = %f\n&quot;, xfun (0.1, {5,1}, {6,2}));
}
</code></pre>
<p>For doing this, we have to provide to <code>xfun</code> variadic arguments that are <code>std::initializer_list</code> objects:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...T&gt;
double xfun (double p, std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;...l);
</code></pre>
<p>Inside <code>xfun</code> implementation, we can convert this list as an <code>std::array</code>:</p>
<pre><code>std::array&lt;Coeff,sizeof...(l)&gt; arr = {Coeff(l)...};
</code></pre>
<p>where we have added a constructor for <code>Coeff</code> that takes as input a <code>std::initializer_list</code>:</p>
<pre><code>struct Coeff  {
   Coeff (std::initializer_list&lt;int&gt; l) {
       // no constexpr 'size' for std::initializer_list in c++11 :(
       if (l.size()!=2)  { throw; }
       t  = *(l.begin()+0);
       ex = *(l.begin()+1);
   }
   int t;
   int ex;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Then the full code of <code>xfun</code> is as follows:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...T&gt;
double xfun (double p, std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;...l)  {
    // We build an array of Coeff object from the initializer lists.
    std::array&lt;Coeff,sizeof...(l)&gt; arr = {Coeff(l)...};
    double result=0.0;
    for (auto const&amp; c : arr) { result += c.t*std::pow(p,c.ex); }
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/11bsEfhn8" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>Still, having the coefficients in a vector as @Jarod42 proposed seems a better idea for many reasons (data centralization, reuse,...)</p>
